Ecuador's shrimp sector faces growing challenges as heightened security risks cast a shadow over its resilience and long-term prosperity, exploding this week in multiple incidents.

"We ended 2023 with a total of 77 criminal incidents against the shrimp sector, resulting in 58 injuries and four fatalities, but we started 2024 even worse, with extremely high levels of insecurity across all regions of the country," a mid-sized Ecuadorian shrimp producer, who spoke on condition of anonymity, told Undercurrent News this week. 

"The situation we face now is terrifying. I know we often say this but now we leave home not knowing if we will return. We make sure all our workers take the utmost precautions but we are not safe. Not even at home. It is impossible to work and, frankly, it is almost impossible to live."

The Latin American country is currently facing the culmination of a long-standing security crisis, reaching its highest peak in recent days, further sources in the industry told Undercurrent. 

Just two months into his presidency, Daniel Noboa, Ecuador's youngest-ever president, has found himself grappling with a sudden surge of violence sweeping across the nation.

On Monday (Jan. 8), Noboa declared a state of emergency to accompany a curfew. Instead of tensions being quelled, however, Ecuador has witnessed explosions, abductions of police officers, vehicles set ablaze and gunfire erupting in key areas, including the capital city of Quito and other regions like Esmeraldas and Cuenca.

Guayaquil, the nation's largest city, has borne the brunt of the crisis, experiencing the most severe incidents. The death toll has risen to at least 10 there, local media reports. 

On Tuesday (Jan. 9), masked and armed men stormed into a television station in Guayaquil during a live broadcast. Before being subdued and apprehended by the authorities, the intruders took the presenters and staff hostage, engaging in gunfire with the police.

Image is still from NBC News video shows 13 men arrested on charges of being involved in an attack on a television station in Guayaquil, Ecuador.

"The uncertainty of the situation and its duration is deeply concerning. Many of us [producers] are unable to carry out their work in a normal manner. The impact of this is extensive and will be substantial for everyone, regardless of our farms' size," a second Ecuadorian shrimp producer from Manabi said. 

"We urged our workers to avoid nighttime trips to our farms as well as vary their routes when coming in. It is not even safe to wear T-shirts or clothing with logos that reveal your workplace now. In fact, we have asked them all to avoid putting them on, just like anything of value," a third Ecuadorian shrimp farmer from Guayas pointed out. 

Further advisory includes the following key recommendations, he shared:

Avoid visiting gas stations, pharmacies, supermarkets and other establishments after nightfall;
When patronizing supermarkets, ensure company and inspect surrounding vehicles for suspicious activities before entry;
Refrain from nighttime road travel. In case of a collision, continue driving without stopping;
If a vehicle obstructs the path while driving, do not halt. Instead, collide with it, preferably in the rear section between the rear tire and the trunk. Accelerate and avoid braking;
Do not leave valuables in the car, including glasses, briefcases, purses, backpacks, cell phones, laptops, packages or bags;
Always secure car doors upon entry and keep personal items out of sight;
Diversify travel routes to work, school, university, or other destinations;
Exercise caution when receiving items from strangers at the doorstep; request them to pass packages or invitations under the door to minimize potential risks;
Ensure doors and gates of the residence are always securely closed;
Avoid disputes or arguments while on the road;
Refrain from disclosing the workplace or residence on social media; delay posting current locations, such as restaurants, until after departure;
Acknowledge vulnerability within a three-block radius of the home or workplace. If faced with an attempted abduction or stoppage, do not comply. Once beyond this range, escape routes become limited; and
 If a vehicle stops beside and a firearm is displayed, do not exit the vehicle. Accelerate and drive away, as the intention may be abduction or car theft.

